# Line-ending policy
#
# Several tests assert against the text of the source they read from the working
# tree (index.html, js/app.js, the deploy workflow YAML, the Python builders).
# Without an explicit policy, what those tests see depends on each developer's
# core.autocrlf / core.eol setting, so the same commit can pass on CI and fail
# on a Windows checkout.
#
#   text=auto  Git detects text vs binary per file and normalises text to LF on
#              commit. Detection leaves the tracked PNGs byte-for-byte intact,
#              so no explicit binary rules are needed.
#   eol=lf     Pins the checkout side as well, which text=auto alone leaves to
#              local configuration. Every platform then gets LF in the working
#              tree, matching the "\n" the data builders already write.
#
# One deliberate consequence: assets/leaflet/leaflet.css and
# assets/leaflet/LICENSE were committed with CRLF and are renormalised to LF by
# this change. That is a line-ending-only rewrite (git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol
# reports no change), it is inert for CSS and licence text, and it brings the
# vendored copies into line with upstream Leaflet, which ships LF. Exempting
# them with "-text" was rejected: it would instead have committed this
# working tree's CRLF for leaflet.js, which the repository stores as LF.
#
# No file here requires CRLF: the repository has no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 scripts.
* text=auto eol=lf
